<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moquegua</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moquegua is a city and region in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, in a warm valley on the western slope of the Andes. In the Middle Horizon the valley was a meeting ground of two Andean states, with Tiwanaku colonies on the valley floor and the Wari stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul">Cerro Baúl</a> above it, and later the Lupaca kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> kept farming colonies there. Under Spanish rule it became a centre of wine and brandy production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moquegua.jpg" alt="Moquegua" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chalisimo5 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plazamoquegua.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Moquegua region was occupied for thousands of years by successive indigenous cultures. The Wari built numerous monuments and terraced fields for hillside cultivation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul">Cerro Baúl</a>, on a hilltop outside the city, is the remains of a Wari monumental site. There are also numerous Tiwanaku sites, and the area was an important colonial centre for the Tiwanaku state. It is the only area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> with both Wari and Tiwanaku ruins. The Chiribaya culture flourished in the Moquegua area and near the Pacific coast from about 1000 until 1360.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-garcilaso-de-la-vega">Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> emperor Mayta Cápac organised the military expedition that took Inca rule to the shore of the Pacific. The Inca overcame the peoples of the region in the 15th century, and in the next century the Spanish conquered them. The Spanish founded the city as Villa de Santa Catalina de Guadalcázar del Valle de Moquegua. Nothing definite is known of the year, but tradition holds that Pedro Cansino and his wife Josefina de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> founded it on 25 November 1541.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Moquegua is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Moquegua, of Mariscal Nieto Province and of Moquegua District, and lies 1,144 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. Its economy rests largely on mining, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, gold and molybdenum; the Cuajone mine and part of the Toquepala mine are in the province, and a copper smelter and refinery in Ilo province treat their concentrates. The Pan-American Highway passes through the city. Its Plaza de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a>, said to have been designed in the 19th century by Gustave Eiffel, holds the José Carlos Mariátegui Museum and the Contisuyo Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moquegua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maize and other warm-valley products came from lower zones: Tiwanaku settlers founded colonies in the <b>Moquegua</b> valley of southern Peru, Tiwanaku pottery spread into the valleys of Cochabamba, and snuff trays carved with Tiwanaku imagery appeared in burials at San Pedro de Atacama in northern Chile from the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tiwanaku">Tiwanaku · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On Cerro Baúl in the <b>Moquegua</b> valley, archaeologists led by Patrick Ryan Williams found a brewery with more than twenty vats, where Wari lords probably hosted feasts that bound local leaders to the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/wari-empire">Wari Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lupaca controlled colonies in the warm Pacific valleys of <b>Moquegua</b> and Sama, where they grew maize and cotton, a pattern called the vertical archipelago.</blockquote><a href="/state/lupaca-kingdom">Lupaca Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
